From 5b63741abf84924fe77c8ecaa70bb6eeb301a4a8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 14:59:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Offer Porphyry houses, and name the polar fallback
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Brief v1 rule 1h has three parts. The Placidus limit at 90° − ε came in the
previous commit. This one adds the other two: a named fallback, and Porphyry
as a system a caller can ask for.

- `houseSystem: "porphyry"` divides each quadrant between the angles into
  three equal arcs of ecliptic longitude. It needs only the ascendant and the
  midheaven, so it is defined at every latitude where they are, inside the
  polar circle included, and never sets `polar-fallback`.
- Placidus keeps whole sign as its polar fallback. That system is now
  exported as `PLACIDUS_POLAR_FALLBACK`, and `computeHouses` returns it as
  `fallbackSystem`. Swiss Ephemeris falls back to Porphyry there instead; the
  README says so, and says how to ask for Porphyry.
- A supplied Chart's houses must be the system its input asked for, or whole
  sign where Placidus fell back. Before, only whole-sign input with Placidus
  houses was refused.
- Receipts accept Porphyry only in the current conventions set, since rc.3 to
  rc.6 never offered it, and check that its cusps trisect the quadrants.

Measured here against Swiss Ephemeris 2.10.03 on Swiss's own inputs, and not
committed: `houses_armc(..., 'O')` at 1,632 cases, 55° to 85° in both
hemispheres, every three hours on 21 June 1800, 2000 and 2200. The engine's
Porphyry cusps agree to 6.1e-9″, and its ascendant is never on the other
side of the horizon. The rule's gate is 0.02″.

porphyry.test.ts holds Porphyry to four of the ERFA anchors in
angles-erfa.test.ts, trisected, within the ascendant's gate. It also checks
that Porphyry computes at four places inside the polar circle while Placidus
falls back there. The file tests the input error, supplied Charts, and the
receipt rules above.

vitest run: 510 of 510.

